          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Like any type constructor in type theory, a function type is specified by rules saying when we can introduce it as a type, how to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term+introduction"} construct terms
            of that type, how to use or “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term+elimination"}> eliminate
            ” terms of that type, and how to compute when we combine the constructors with the eliminators.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+formation"} type formation
            rule to build a function type is easy:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi Type
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi Type
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi Type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{A\colon Type \qquad B \colon Type}{A\to B\colon Type}
          %h3#as_a_negative_type As a negative type
          %p
            Function types are almost always defined as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negative+type"} negative type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+application"} apply
            it to any input:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{f\colon A\to B \qquad a\colon A}{f(a) \colon B}
          %p
            The constructor is then determined as usual for a negative type: to construct a term of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            containing a free variable of type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "-abstraction\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mi λ
                      %mi x
                      %mo .
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{x\colon A\vdash b\colon B}{\lambda x.b\colon A\to B}
          %p
            The
            %span.newWikiWord
              ∞-reduction
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/%E2%88%9E-reduction"} ?
            rule is the obvious one (the ur-example of all
            = succeed "-reductions)," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi β
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
            = succeed "-abstraction," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mo →
                    %mi β
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\lambda x.b)(a) \;\to_\beta\; b[a/x]
          %p
            If we want an
            %span.newWikiWord
              ∞-conversion
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/%E2%88%9E-conversion"} ?
            rule, the natural one says that every function is a
            = succeed "-abstraction:" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mo →
                    %mi η
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda x.f(x) \;\to_\eta\; f
          %h3#AsPositiveType As a positive type
          %p
            It is also possible to present function types as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+type"} positive type
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+framework"} logical framework
            = succeed "-abstraction)," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi C
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mi funsplit
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo ,
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{(x\colon A \vdash b\colon B) \vdash c\colon C \qquad f\colon A\to B}{funsplit(c,f)\colon C}
          %p
            This rule cannot be formulated in the usual presentation of type theory, since it involves a “higher-order judgment”: the context of the term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c\colon C
            involves a “term of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            containing a free variable of type
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            to depend on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to B
          %p
            The natural
            = succeed "-reduction" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi β
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi funsplit
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
