          %p
            Strict function types appear in most dependent type theories such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+type+theory"} Martin-Löf type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observational+type+theory"} observational type theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+type+theory"} cubical type theory
          %p
            For strict function types, the judgmental computation and uniqueness rules automatically imply the propositional computation and uniqueness rules, as by the rules for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgmental+equality"} judgmental equality
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
          %h3#as_types_of_anafunctions As types of anafunctions
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+types"} equivalence types
            as types of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/one-to-one+correspondences"} one-to-one correspondences
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anafunctions"} anafunctions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indexed+heterogeneous+identity+types"} indexed heterogeneous identity types
            being defined first, which we shall write as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a =_A b
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %msubsup
                    %mo =
                    %mi B
                    %mi p
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x =_{B}^{p} y
            respectively for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b:A
            = succeed "," do
